+# read_content oracle service
+
+Generic **file &rarr; Qubic** oracle. A contract names a file by the **SHA-256 of its bytes**; the
+service fetches it from IPFS gateways, Arweave gateways or content mirrors, checks the hash, and
+returns its size, a byte range (up to 512 bytes) or one JSON field. Contracts keep only a fingerprint
+on-chain and read the file's content when they need it.
+
+Implemented in Python (`read_content_service.py`, stdlib only), with the same OM node <-> service
+framing as `read_evm_log`.
+
+## Wire format
+
+Mirrors core's `OI::ContentRead` (`src/oracle_interfaces/ContentRead.h`, interface index 7).
+
+### Query — 208 bytes
+
+| offset | size | field         | notes                                                       |
+|-------:|-----:|---------------|-------------------------------------------------------------|
+| 0      | 32   | `sha256`      | SHA-256 of the whole file: decides which bytes are accepted |
+| 32     | 8    | `locatorKind` | 0 none (mirrors only), 1 IPFS CID, 2 Arweave id             |
+| 40     | 8    | `locatorLen`  | 0 for none; 1..64                                           |
+| 48     | 64   | `locator`     | CID (`[A-Za-z0-9]`) or 43-char Arweave id; **only a hint**  |
+| 112    | 8    | `maxFileSize` | 1..64 MiB; larger files are never downloaded; sets the fee  |
+| 120    | 8    | `mode`        | 1 size, 2 byte range, 3 JSON field                          |
+| 128    | 8    | `offset`      | mode 2 only, else 0                                         |
+| 136    | 8    | `length`      | mode 2: 1..512, else 0                                      |
+| 144    | 64   | `jsonPath`    | mode 3: dot path, e.g. `meta.licence`, `items.3.name`; empty = root |
+
+Fields a mode doesn't use must be zero, and bytes after `locatorLen` / the path must be zero, so equal
+requests are equal bytes.
+
+### Reply — 536 bytes
+
+| offset | size | field      | notes                                                         |
+|-------:|-----:|------------|---------------------------------------------------------------|
+| 0      | 8    | `code`     | result code                                                   |
+| 8      | 8    | `fileSize` | size of the verified file                                     |
+| 16     | 8    | `dataLen`  | 0..512                                                        |
+| 24     | 512  | `data`     | mode 2: the bytes; mode 3: the value's RFC 8785 canonical JSON |
+
+On any non-zero `code`, **all value fields are zero**.
+
+## Result codes
+
+| code | name              | meaning                                                              |
+|-----:|-------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------|
+| 0    | `SUCCESS`         | file verified; reply fields valid                                    |
+| 1    | `BAD_QUERY`       | malformed query (see above)                                          |
+| 2    | `NOT_FOUND`       | every source answered and none delivered matching bytes within `maxFileSize` (retriable) |
+| 3    | `BAD_RANGE`       | `offset + length` past the end of the file                           |
+| 4    | `NOT_JSON`        | not strict I-JSON (RFC 7493): invalid UTF-8, BOM, duplicate keys, NaN... |
+| 5    | `NO_FIELD`        | the path doesn't exist (array indexes must be canonical: `3`, not `03`) |
+| 6    | `VALUE_TOO_LARGE` | the value's canonical JSON is over 512 bytes                         |
+
+## Determinism guarantees
+
+- **The hash decides which bytes are accepted.** Bytes that don't hash to `sha256` are discarded and the next source is
+  tried, so a lying gateway can waste time but never change an answer. Every machine that answers
+  holds the same bytes.
+- **Sizes and ranges** follow directly from the verified bytes.
+- **JSON** is parsed strictly as I-JSON and returned in RFC 8785 (JCS) canonical form: sorted keys
+  (by UTF-16 code units), no whitespace, ECMAScript number formatting, so `3.0` and `3` both become `3`
+  on every machine. The implementation passes the RFC's own example byte-for-byte.
+- **Missing vs failing.** A source that answers 404/410, serves wrong bytes or serves more than
+  `maxFileSize` is a **miss**. A timeout, connection error, 5xx or 429 is an **error**. If nothing
+  matched and any source errored, the service **abstains** rather than claim `NOT_FOUND`.
+- **An oversized file is `NOT_FOUND`**, never "too large": it can't be verified without downloading it
+  all, so the oracle never asserts anything about it.
+
+## Config
+
+| env var                          | purpose                                                         |
+|----------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------|
+| `READ_CONTENT_SERVICE_HOST/PORT` | interface slot the OM node reaches this service on (default port 9009) |
+| `CONTENT_IPFS_GATEWAYS`          | comma-separated bases, fetched as `<base>/ipfs/<cid>`; put your **own** IPFS node first |
+| `CONTENT_ARWEAVE_GATEWAYS`       | comma-separated bases, fetched as `<base>/<id>`                  |
+| `CONTENT_MIRRORS`                | URL templates containing `{sha256}` (lowercase hex)              |
+| `CONTENT_FETCH_TIMEOUT_S`        | per-source timeout, default 20                                  |
+
+The service never builds a URL from anything but the operator's configured bases and a locator that
+passed the character checks above.
+
+Run: `python3 read_content_service.py` (Python 3.8+, no third-party packages).
+
+## Tests
+
+```bash
+python3 test/mock_gateway.py --port 18845 &
+python3 test/mock_gateway.py --port 18846 --tamper &
+python3 test/mock_gateway.py --port 18847 --broken &
+python3 test/selftest.py     # exit 0 = all scenarios pass (45 checks)
+```
+
+Covers:
+
+- **Every mode:** size, byte range, JSON field.
+- **Every source type:** IPFS, Arweave, a mirror found by hash.
+- **Bad sources:** a lying gateway is skipped in favour of an honest one; when only wrong bytes are available, the answer is `NOT_FOUND`.
+- **Size limit:** a 3 MiB file is refused at 2 MiB and served at 3 MiB.
+- **Result codes:** every one.
+- **Malformed queries:** 14 kinds, including path characters in a CID.
+- **Abstaining:** a failed gateway that would otherwise have hidden the file.
+- **Canonical JSON:** RFC 8785's example and number vectors.
+- **The TCP framing.**
+
+Against live gateways (nothing is sent to Qubic):
+
+```bash
+CONTENT_IPFS_GATEWAYS=https://ipfs.io,https://dweb.link \
+python3 test/live_check.py --cid <cid> --sha256 <sha256sum of the file> --json meta.licence
+```
